     1	# Exact model and certification derivation
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-10-14 (PT). Evidence label: Certification (subject to independent review); novelty is not claimed.
     4	
     5	## Scope
     6	
     7	The book asks to minimize pixel expansion, maximize contrast, maximize contrast at minimum expansion, and minimize expansion at maximum contrast for general black-and-white visual cryptography. Its cited primary source is Ateniese, Blundo, De Santis, Stinson (1996), Definitions 2.1 and 2.2. These are different models.
     8	
     9	We enumerate every nonconstant **strong** monotone access structure on n participants: a nonempty antichain A of nonempty minimal qualified sets; Q is its upward closure; F is the complement in 2^[n]. Dummy participants and singleton-qualified sets are included. All qualified sets, not only A, must reconstruct by stacking all their shares. Weak structures with unclassified sets, XOR reconstruction, reversing, meaningful cover shares, random grids, and expected-only contrast are not the enumeration's model.
    10	
    11	For a binary n-by-m matrix M, W_Q(M) counts columns with at least one black entry in rows Q. A deterministic VCS still randomly selects an encoding matrix; “deterministic” means an almost-sure separation, not an absence of encoding randomness. In the collection model, there are finite multisets C0,C1, uniform sampling, identical distributions of the entire F-row restriction for every F in F, and an integer gap
    12	
    13	    g = min_{Q in Q} [min_{M in C1} W_Q(M) - max_{M in C0} W_Q(M)] >= 1.
    14	
    15	We optimize alpha=g/m. Ateniese et al. call alpha the “relative difference” and g the “contrast”; later papers commonly call alpha “contrast.” Maximizing unnormalized g without bounding m is unbounded by repetition, so the book's maximum-contrast objective is interpreted as alpha. No Michelson or background-normalized alternative is substituted.
    16	
    17	The basis-matrix subclass chooses all column permutations of fixed B0,B1. In this class the two F-row column multisets must coincide; each qualified weight is fixed. The four finite-expansion optima below are initially certified only in this subclass. They must not be silently promoted to the collection model.
    18	
    19	## Column-count LP and ILPs
    20	
    21	Index a column by T subset [n], its set of black rows. Let x_T,y_T be the numbers of copies in B0,B1. Both sums are m. For S subset [n] define
    22	
    23	    f(S) = sum_{T intersect S = empty} (x_T-y_T).
    24	
    25	Then f(empty)=0 and W_Q(B1)-W_Q(B0)=f(Q). Security is exactly f(F)=0 for all F in F. To see sufficiency, the difference of counts of any prescribed pattern on F (zero rows Z and one rows U, Z disjoint U, Z union U=F) equals
    26	
    27	    sum_{V subset U} (-1)^|V| f(Z union V),
    28	
    29	which vanishes because F is downward closed. Necessity follows by inspecting the all-zero projected pattern. Thus only column multiplicities matter for a fixed basis pair.
    30	
    31	The integer programs have x,y nonnegative integers:
    32	
    33	1. Minimum m: minimize sum x subject to sum x=sum y, f(F)=0, f(Q)>=1.
    34	2. Maximum g at that m: maximize integer g subject to the same equalities, sums=m, and f(Q)>=g.
    35	3. Maximum alpha: allow rational x,y, set both sums=1, maximize a with f(Q)>=a. This is a bounded rational LP.
    36	4. Minimum m at alpha*=p/q reduced: minimize sum x subject to equal sums, f(F)=0, q f(Q)>=p sum x, and sum x>=1.
    37	
    38	The rational LP optimum is attained. Clearing denominators gives integer basis matrices attaining the same alpha, so its value is the exact maximum over all finite basis schemes, not merely a supremum. Every LP primal and dual is checked using Fraction arithmetic; floating-point discovery is not itself a certificate.
    39	
    40	## Maximum relative contrast is unchanged by allowing collections
    41	
    42	Take any finite collection scheme, and replicate matrices so both collections have the same size r. Concatenate all r matrices in C0, and separately all r matrices in C1. Equality of forbidden projected-matrix distributions implies equality of aggregated projected-column counts, hence these concatenations form a basis pair. For every qualified Q, the black concatenation weight minus the white one is at least r*g. Its expansion is r*m, so its relative contrast is at least g/m. Conversely every basis pair defines a collection scheme. Therefore the global maximum relative contrast is identical in the two models. This argument does **not** preserve minimum expansion, or expansion at the contrast optimum.
    43	
    44	Consequently, every collection scheme satisfies m >= ceil(1/alpha*), and its integer gap at expansion m is at most floor(alpha* m). When an integer basis witness matches either bound, that particular optimum transfers to collections. If alpha*=p/q, any finite scheme attaining it has q dividing m; equality of the realized worst-case integer gap g/m supplies this divisibility.
    45	
    46	## Independent integer formulation
    47	
    48	Let d_T=x_T-y_T. The complement-zeta transform above has the exact inverse
    49	
    50	    d_T = sum_{S superset ([n] minus T)} (-1)^{|S|-|[n] minus T|} f(S).
    51	
    52	All f(S) are integers, f(F)=0, and f(Q)>=g. Since f(empty)=0, sum d=0, so the minimum length realizing d is
    53	
    54	    m(d) = sum_T max(d_T,0) = (1/2) sum_T |d_T|.
    55	
    56	Conversely x=max(d,0), y=max(-d,0) realize d. Common columns can be added to reach any larger common expansion without changing f. A second exact integer solver uses only integer f(Q), the inversion formula, and a sum-of-positive-parts length bound. It never imports the generator's constraint matrix. It checks infeasibility below each claimed m, above each claimed fixed-m gap, and below each claimed expansion attaining alpha*. Rational LP bounds and denominator divisibility may discharge such obligations without a solver.
    57	
    58	## General trade-off consequences
    59	
    60	Write G(m) for the largest integer gap at expansion m, setting G(m)=0 if no positive-gap scheme exists (identical matrices always give zero). Padding proves G(m+1)>=G(m). Concatenation proves G(m1+m2)>=G(m1)+G(m2). The LP gives G(m)<=floor(alpha* m). If an optimum-alpha basis witness has expansion L and gap h, repetition and padding give
    61	
    62	    h floor(m/L) <= G(m) <= floor(h m/L).
    63	
    64	In particular G(kL)=kh, and G(m)/m tends to alpha*. These elementary statements do not claim a closed formula at the remaining residue classes, nor novelty. The tables test whether minimum expansion also attains alpha*; one contrary structure refutes that universal assertion, but is not automatically a counterexample to any published conjecture.

